+5. **Anything that locks and then flaps** — the camera, the segmentation
+   boundary — with a note about what was happening on screen.
+
+The first four are bugs. The fifth is tuning, and it is the kind that cannot be
+guessed at from a machine with no GPU.
diff --git a/v3/docs/INPUT-PIPELINE.md b/v3/docs/INPUT-PIPELINE.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9d5f928
--- /dev/null
+++ b/v3/docs/INPUT-PIPELINE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,389 @@
+# The complete input pipeline — and where to beat the state of the art
+
+## Scope and honesty about sources
+
+What is publicly known about the existing paid solution in this space: GTA V
+does not output engine motion vectors, and the mod patches a large number of the
+game's shaders to inject them. Its own changelogs describe follow-up fixes for
+cases where vehicles or roads stopped producing correct motion vectors in
+combination with graphics mods such as QuantV and NVE.
+
+Everything in this document about *that* implementation is therefore limited to
+what its authors have stated publicly. The analysis below is about the
+**structural properties of the approach**, which follow from the technique itself
+and are true regardless of how well any particular version implements it. Where
+that distinction matters, it is marked.
+
+It is also possible that some of the weaknesses described here have since been
+addressed in builds this document's author has not seen. The point is not to
+score against another project; it is that a different architecture makes some of
+these problems impossible rather than fixable.
+
+Nothing here requires or permits copying code, binaries, shader patches or
+authentication from anyone. Reimplementing a technique from its public
+description is legitimate; lifting an implementation is not.
+
+---
+
+## 1. The structural weakness worth attacking
+
+The approach is: find every shader that draws scene geometry, patch it to emit
+motion vectors, feed those to the generator.
+
+Its failure mode follows directly from that sentence. **Coverage has to be
+complete, and it is never permanently complete.** Every game patch and every
+graphics mod introduces shaders the patcher has not seen. A shader that is missed
+produces no motion vectors for whatever it draws. A shader that is mis-patched
+produces wrong ones.
+
+And the consequence of a gap is severe, because the generator has no way to know
+it is looking at a hole. Missing motion reads as *zero* motion, which the
+generator interprets as "this pixel is stationary" — so a moving vehicle with an
+unpatched shader does not merely lose quality, it smears. The published history of
+per-mod motion-vector fixes is this failure mode showing up in the field.
+
+Call this **failing open**: when coverage breaks, the output is garbage, and the
+only repair is to extend the patcher again.
+
+### The inversion
+
+Reconstruct motion from depth and the camera matrices as the **base layer**, and
+let per-object geometry motion **override** it where it exists.
+
+```
+        depth + camera matrices          patched shaders
+                 │                              │
+                 ▼                              ▼
+       reprojected motion  ────merge────►  final motion
+       (exact for the static world)    (correct for movers too)
+```
+
+Now consider the same failure. A shader that is missed contributes nothing, so
+its pixels keep the reprojected value — which says "this moves the way the world
+moves". For a lamp post, a building or a road surface that is **exactly right**.
+For a moving vehicle it is wrong, but wrong in a bounded, local way rather than
+catastrophically.
+
+Call this **failing closed**: when coverage breaks, the output degrades toward
+"treated as scenery", which is correct for the overwhelming majority of the frame.
+
+Two consequences follow, and they are the reason this is worth the effort:
+
+**You no longer need to patch everything.** Static world geometry is already
+exact from reprojection. The shaders that must be patched are the ones drawing
+things that move independently — vehicles, pedestrians, animated props. That is
+tens of shaders rather than hundreds, and it is a set you can enumerate and
+verify rather than a set you have to chase.
+
+**A new game patch degrades instead of breaking.** New scene shaders inherit the
+reprojection base automatically. Only newly-introduced *movers* need attention,
+and their absence is a localised artifact, not a broken frame.
+
+This is implemented in the arbitration layer as of this version:
+
+```cpp
+enum class MotionSource {
+  None, OpticalFlow, CameraReprojected, Hybrid, Geometry
+};
+constexpr bool MayDriveFrameGeneration(MotionSource s) {
+  return s == MotionSource::Geometry;
+}
+```
+
+```
+tests/test_arbitration.cpp
+  ok   camera_motion_is_preferred_over_estimated_motion
+  ok   camera_motion_alone_is_used_when_there_is_nothing_to_combine_it_with
+  ok   geometry_motion_outranks_every_other_source
+  ok   only_geometry_motion_may_ever_drive_generated_frames
+  ok   camera_motion_does_not_unlock_generated_frames
+  ok   invariants_hold_across_the_entire_input_space   (1,728 cases)
+```
+
+Note the last two carefully. The base layer makes the *image pass* better
+immediately, and it is still **not** allowed to drive generated frames, because
+it cannot see independent movement. Improving the fallback must not lower the bar
+for the generator — that would trade one honest limitation for a subtle one.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Trusting motion vectors is a mistake; validate them
+
+Even a correct patch can produce a wrong vector. The hard part of per-object
+motion is not emitting it, it is knowing the object's **previous** transform,
+which means recognising that this draw call is the same object as one from last
+frame. Any identity heuristic — hashing index and vertex buffers, hashing the
+object's constant buffer — will occasionally collide or miss. A collision
+produces a confidently wrong vector, which is worse than no vector at all.
+
+So do not trust the buffer. **Check it.**
+
+After motion is assembled, run one cheap compute pass: for each pixel, sample the
+previous frame's colour at the position the motion vector points to, and compare
+it to the current colour over a small neighbourhood. Where the error is large,
+the vector did not predict what actually happened, and it is not trustworthy.
+
+That error field is worth two things:
+
+- **Fallback.** Replace low-confidence per-object vectors with the reprojected
+  base, which is at least self-consistent.
+- **A reactive mask.** Hand the confidence field to DLSS as its reactive or
+  disocclusion input. This is what those inputs exist for, and it is also the
+  correct answer for the class of surfaces that can never have good motion
+  vectors at all — glass, particles, rain, alpha-tested foliage — because they
+  have no single depth or single motion to describe.
+
+This pass costs a fraction of a millisecond and converts the most dangerous
+failure mode (confidently wrong) into the mildest one (known-uncertain).
+
+It also gives the interface something honest to display, which no closed solution
+can: *how much of this frame's motion do we actually believe?*
+
+---
+
+## 3. Alignment between NR and FG, by construction
+
+Running neural rendering and frame generation together is not a matter of
+enabling both. They must consume **the same inputs from the same frame**. If the
+image pass reads depth from frame N and the generator reads motion assembled for
+frame N−1, the result is temporal disagreement that looks like a rendering bug
+and is extremely hard to attribute.
+
+The usual way this goes wrong is that each consumer grabs the resources it needs
+independently, and alignment holds by convention — until a resolution change, a
+device reset or a dropped frame breaks it.
+
+**Make it structural instead.** The provider publishes one immutable frame packet:
+
+```
+FramePacket {
+  frame_index          monotonic, never reused
+  depth                + its convention
+  motion               + its MotionSource and confidence field
+  hudless colour
+  ui colour + alpha
+  camera constants     current and previous, jitter offset
+}
+```
+
+Consumers do not gather resources. They ask for a packet, and a packet either
+satisfies a consumer's requirements or it does not:
+
+- the image pass requires depth, motion of any source, and constants
+- the generator additionally requires HUD-less colour, the UI layer, and motion
+  whose source is `Geometry`
+
+A consumer handed a packet that does not satisfy it **declines the frame** rather
+than running on partial data. Alignment is then not something to maintain; it is
+a property of there being exactly one packet per frame index.
+
+This is what the existing `shared_neural_contract` flag should come to mean: not
+"the provider says it is fine", but "one packet satisfied both consumers".
+
+---
+
+## 4. The interface, and a FiveM-specific advantage
+
+Generic GTA V solutions handle the HUD by capturing the scene before the
+interface is drawn. That is necessary and not sufficient, for three reasons.
+
+**Transparent elements.** A translucent HUD panel is neither scene nor interface;
+it is both. Capturing "before UI" leaves the scene underneath correct, which is
+right — but the panel itself must then be composited at full resolution *after*
+generation, never passed through it.
+
+**World-space interface.** Markers, nameplates and waypoints are drawn in screen
+space but *belong* to world positions. They must move with the camera. Excluding
+them entirely makes them judder against the world; passing them through unmarked
+makes them smear. They need camera motion specifically — the reprojection base
+already computes exactly that, and this is a case where having it pays off twice.
+
+**And the part that is specific to your target:** on a FiveM RP server, most of
+the interface a player sees is not GTA's HUD at all. It is the server's own UI,
+drawn through NUI — a browser surface composited into the frame. That is a
+distinct, consistent render path, which makes it both easier to detect than
+GTA's own HUD draws and more important to get right, because on a busy RP server
+it covers a large fraction of the screen.
+
+A solution built for singleplayer GTA V has no reason to handle this and, as far
+as public information goes, does not. For your target it is arguably the single
+highest-value difference available — and it is *easier* than the general problem,
+not harder.
+
+The practical rule, in order of when each is applied:
+
+```
+scene  →  motion/depth  →  generator  →  composite UI at full resolution
+                                          ├─ screen-space UI: never entered the generator
+                                          └─ world-space markers: camera motion, generated
+```
+
+---
+
+## 5. Upscaling: resolution, and the fight you must not have
+
+Two ways to render below native:
+
+**Resize the scene render targets.** Intercept creation of scene-sized targets
+and allocate them smaller. Correct, and invasive: every post-process step that
+assumes a fixed relationship between buffer sizes breaks, and anything sampling
+with hard-coded texel offsets misbehaves.
+
+**Render into a sub-rectangle of full-size targets.** Keep allocations native,
+constrain viewport and scissor. Resource descriptors stay stable, resolution
+changes cost nothing, and device-loss handling gets simpler. It wastes memory
+bandwidth on the unused region, and anything that samples the full extent still
+needs care.
+
+Which is better is an empirical question about how GTA's post chain is built.
+Prototype the second first — it is far cheaper to abandon.
+
+**The fight you must not have.** DLSS super resolution is a temporal accumulator.
+So is the game's own temporal anti-aliasing. Two accumulators consuming the same
+frames, with different jitter assumptions and different histories, produce
+smearing that neither one is responsible for. **The game's temporal AA has to be
+off** whenever DLSS-SR is running.
+
+This is the same principle the arbitration layer already encodes for motion:
+two temporal systems must not silently fight for the same job. Worth extending
+the same rule to the upscaler rather than discovering it as a bug.
+
+**Jitter** must be injected into the projection matrix before the constant buffer
+is uploaded, and the *same* offsets handed to DLSS. Two failure modes to watch:
+a pass that rebuilds the matrix itself and so never sees the jitter, and a pass
+that should not be jittered at all — shadow rendering uses its own matrices and
+must be left alone.
+
+---
+
+## 6. The D3D11 ↔ D3D12 crossing
+
+Frame generation requires D3D12 or Vulkan; the game is D3D11. The interop layer
+is unavoidable, and it is where the performance goes if it is done carelessly.
+
+**Cross as little as possible.** Depth, HUD-less colour and the UI layer have to
+cross. Motion does not: it can be *generated* on the D3D12 side from depth and
+the camera constants, which are a handful of bytes. Per-object contributions
+still have to cross, but they are sparse.
+
+**Never synchronise with flushes.** Use shared fences
+(`ID3D11Device5::CreateFence` with the shared flag, opened on D3D12). A
+flush-and-hope design appears to work and then produces a torn frame under load,
+several frames after the actual mistake.
+
+**Use a ring of shared resources.** A single shared texture per input serialises
+the two devices on each other. Three deep is usually enough to keep both busy.
+
+**Build the repro harness before the feature.** A synchronisation bug surfaces as
+corruption some frames later, and without a deterministic way to reproduce it you
+will be debugging by intuition. This is the stage where that costs the most time.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Brittleness, and why runtime detection is not optional
+
